.\" Man page for Plica. Read in place with: man -l docs/plica.1
.TH PLICA 1 "2026-10-30" "Plica 0.5.22" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
plica \- a mouse-friendly terminal text editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B plica
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fB+\fILINE\fR] [\fIFILE\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B plica
is a text editor for the terminal that works like a desktop editor:
typing edits straight away, the usual shortcuts work (Ctrl+S, Ctrl+Z,
Ctrl+F, ...), and the mouse clicks, drags, scrolls and presses buttons.
.PP
Each \fIFILE\fR opens in its own tab; a file that doesn't exist yet is
created when first saved.
Markdown tables and bordered plain-text tables are edited cell by cell,
words and phrases can be highlighted without changing the file, and the
colours can follow a Kitty-format colour file live.
.PP
Press \fBF1\fR inside Plica for the keys and \fBF9\fR for Preferences.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI + LINE
Start with the caret on \fILINE\fR of the first file.
.TP
.BI \-\-palette " PATH"
Follow a Kitty-format colour file, reloading it whenever it changes.
.TP
.BI \-\-theme " NAME"
Use a built-in theme: \fBdracula\fR, \fBgruvbox\fR, \fBnord\fR,
\fBsolarized-dark\fR, \fBsolarized-light\fR, or \fBterminal\fR (the
terminal's own colours).
.TP
.B \-\-builtin\-theme
Same as \fB\-\-theme dracula\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-encoding " NAME"
Read the files as \fINAME\fR (for example \fBwindows-1252\fR,
\fBshift_jis\fR, \fButf-16le\fR) instead of detecting their encoding.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Use this config file instead of the default.
.TP
.B \-\-print\-config
Print a commented default config and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-print\-keys
Print every key, with the bindings from your config, as a Markdown table,
and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-config
Check the config file, list any problems and exit (status 1 if there are any).
.TP
.BI \-\-check\-palette " PATH"
Parse a colour file, print the resolved colours and contrast ratios (interface, highlights and syntax colours), and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
Treat every following argument as a file name.
.SH KEYS
The usual desktop keys: \fBCtrl+S\fR save, \fBCtrl+Shift+S\fR or \fBF12\fR
save as, \fBCtrl+O\fR open, \fBCtrl+N\fR new tab, \fBCtrl+W\fR close tab,
\fBCtrl+Q\fR quit, \fBCtrl+Z\fR / \fBCtrl+Y\fR undo / redo,
\fBCtrl+C\fR / \fBCtrl+X\fR / \fBCtrl+V\fR copy / cut / paste,
\fBCtrl+A\fR select all, \fBCtrl+F\fR find, \fBCtrl+H\fR replace,
\fBF3\fR / \fBShift+F3\fR next / previous match, \fBCtrl+G\fR go to line,
\fBCtrl+PageUp\fR / \fBCtrl+PageDown\fR previous / next tab,
\fBAlt+1\fR ... \fBAlt+9\fR go to a tab.
.PP
\fBAlt+Z\fR soft wrap, \fBAlt+L\fR line numbers, \fBAlt+W\fR show
whitespace, \fBAlt+M\fR give the mouse to the terminal,
\fBAlt+H\fR or \fBF8\fR highlight the word in this document,
\fBAlt+E\fR or \fBF7\fR encoding menu, \fBF4\fR language for syntax colours,
\fBAlt+T\fR or \fBF6\fR table menu, \fBAlt+R\fR recent files,
\fBF9\fR Preferences, \fBF1\fR help.
.PP
A file opens where it was left: the caret, the scroll position, and a
language or encoding chosen for it by hand.
\fBAlt+R\fR (or the \fBRecent\fR place in the Open dialog) lists recent
files; type to filter it by name or folder.
\fB+LINE\fR on the command line goes to that line instead.
Preferences \(-> Files (\fB[files] remember_position = false\fR) turns this off.
.PP
Open and Save as use a file dialog: places on the left (Recent, Home,
Desktop, Documents, the document's folder, the file system and your
bookmarks), the folder's files with their size and date (click a column
heading to sort), and a preview of the selected file. Typing goes to the
\fBName\fR field: when opening it filters the list, and a name ending in
\fB/\fR goes into that folder, so a whole path can still be typed.
Double-click or \fBEnter\fR opens; \fBBackspace\fR in an empty name or
\fBAlt+Up\fR goes up, \fBAlt+Left\fR / \fBAlt+Right\fR back and forward,
\fBTab\fR moves to the places. \fBCtrl+D\fR bookmarks the folder,
\fBCtrl+N\fR makes a new one, \fBAlt+.\fR (or \fBCtrl+H\fR) shows hidden
files and \fBAlt+P\fR the preview. Save as also chooses the encoding and
line endings (LF or CRLF) to write; changing the line endings is one undo
step.
.PP
\fBInsert\fR, or a click on \fBINS\fR/\fBOVR\fR in the status bar, switches
overwrite mode: typed characters replace the ones after the caret (block
cursor, \fBOVR\fR in the status bar). The line ending is never replaced, and
paste, Backspace, Delete, Tab and Enter work as in insert mode.
.PP
Every command can be rebound in the \fB[keys]\fR section of the config;
Preferences \(-> Keybindings \(-> \fBEdit config.toml\fR opens it in a tab, and
saving it there applies the changes at once.
.B plica \-\-print\-keys
lists them all, including the fixed keys for tables, the find bar and the mouse.
.SH SYNTAX COLOURS
Code and markup are coloured by language (about 200 grammars, the set
.B bat
uses). The language comes from the file name or extension, or from a
\fB#!\fR first line; the status bar shows it, and a click on it (or
\fBF4\fR) chooses another, plain text, or automatic again.
Token colours are the palette's ANSI colours and roles, so they follow the
theme; \fB[syntax.colours]\fR in the config changes them, and
Preferences \(-> Appearance (\fB[syntax] enabled = false\fR) turns them off.
Files over 10 MB are not coloured, nor lines over 8 KB.
.SH TABLES
A table is a Markdown table (a header row followed by a row such as
\fB|---|---|\fR) or a fully bordered plain-text table drawn with
\fB+---+\fR and \fB|\fR or with Unicode box-drawing characters.
Text merely laid out in columns with spaces is never treated as a table.
Preferences \(-> Tables (\fB[tables] enabled = false\fR) turns table
recognition off, so that every table is plain text (for ASCII art).
.PP
In a table, \fBTab\fR / \fBShift+Tab\fR move between cells (Tab in the last
cell adds a row), \fBEnter\fR adds a row, \fBAlt+Up\fR / \fBAlt+Down\fR move
the row, and typing keeps the column lined up.
\fBEsc\fR gives plain keys in that table until pressed again.
The table menu (\fBAlt+T\fR, \fBF6\fR, or a right-click) inserts tables,
adds, deletes and moves rows and columns, aligns columns, converts between
Markdown, ASCII and box tables, and copies a table as tab-separated cells
for a spreadsheet.
Dragging across cells selects them; \fBCtrl+C\fR then copies them as
tab-separated text, and pasting tab-separated text into a table fills it.
.SH ENCODINGS
UTF-8, UTF-16 and about 35 older encodings are detected and written back
unchanged.
Bytes that are not valid in the file's encoding are shown as \fB\exNN\fR
and saved as they were.
If a character can't be stored in the file's encoding, saving stops and
offers UTF-8 instead.
Binary files and UTF-32 are refused.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/plica/config.toml
Settings, highlight groups and key bindings
(default \fI~/.config/plica/config.toml\fR).
Preferences (F9) edits it, keeping comments.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/plica/documents.toml
Highlights for individual documents, by full path. An Untitled
document's highlights are written here when it is first saved.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/plica/bookmarks.toml
Folders bookmarked in the Open / Save as dialog.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/plica/recovery/
Snapshots of unsaved work, offered back after a crash
(default \fI~/.local/state/plica/recovery/\fR).
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/plica/recent.toml
Recent files and where each was left (up to 500; safe to delete).
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kitty/current-theme.conf
The theme chosen with Kitty's theme selector (\fBkitty +kitten themes\fR).
Followed for colours when no theme or palette is configured, if it exists.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PLICA_PALETTE
A colour file to follow, like \fB\-\-palette\fR.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Use the terminal's default colours (when the theme is \fBauto\fR and no
palette is configured).
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " XDG_STATE_HOME
Where the config and the recovery snapshots live.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 if a file can't be opened, an option is wrong, or
\fB\-\-check\-config\fR found problems.
.SH NOTES
Many terminals keep Ctrl+Shift+letter, Ctrl+Tab and Shift+click for
themselves; every such action has a fallback (F12 for Save As, Ctrl+Y for
redo, Alt+click to extend a selection).
With XFCE Terminal's menu bar shown, use F6, F7 and F8 instead of Alt+T,
Alt+E and Alt+H.
In tmux, enable \fBset -g mouse on\fR.
